Hey [~accountid:63c7e845417bef6fc90711eb] thanks for ticket.

A few thoughts:

* In PT we have a legal rebuttable presumption of employment specifically for platform workers (e.g. TVDE drivers) on the basis of 6 specific criteria:
*# _Remuneration_ - The digital platform fixes the remuneration for the work performed on the platform or establishes maximum and minimum limits for that remuneration;
*# _Power of direction_ - The platform exercises power of direction and determines specific rules, in particular with regard to i) the way the activity provider presents itself,  ii) its conduct towards the service user or iii) the provision of the activity;
*# _Control & activity monitoring_ - The platform controls and monitors the provision of the activity, including in real time, or checks the quality of the activity provided, including by electronic means or algorithmic management;
*# _Autonomy re: organisation of work_ - The platform restricts the autonomy of the activity provider regarding the organisation of work, especially regarding: i) the choice of working hours or periods of absence, ii) the possibility of accepting or refusing tasks, iii) the use of subcontractors or substitutes, iv) through the application of sanctions v) the choice of customers or vi) to provide activity to third parties via the platform;
*# _Disciplinary power_ - The platform exercises labour powers over the activity provider, namely disciplinary power, including exclusion from future activities on the platform through account deactivation; 
*# _Work tools_ - The equipment and work instruments used belong to the platform or are operated by them through a lease agreement/rental contract.
* Some courts seem to understand that providing training is one of the powers of direction configuring a relation of subordination between an employee and employer. Hence, training may trigger reclassification risk. Particularly risky would be providing payment directly to drivers of these training or considering them mandatory for the provision of transportation through the platform. 
* Ideally all communications should be made to fleet operators only. Where we still need to address drivers directly as in the case of the events you foresee, please consider the following:
** it should be in a manner that indicates that the driver is “acting as representative of / on behalf of / at the service of” the fleet operator and not an employee of Bolt. Please have a look at the internal guide [https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CONTENT/pages/4468998200/Reclassification+Risk+Copy+Guide|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CONTENT/pages/4468998200/Reclassification+Risk+Copy+Guide|smart-link] that aims to help ensuring a consistent message on this regard.
** the events should not be mandatory;
** fleet owners should be part of the events.
* IMO the proposed events would not appear to trigger special concerns in terms of reclassificaiton.

