Internal summary:

* Bolt Business customer (KPMG) in AZ requested a few privacy clauses in the Special Terms;
* main request - to ensure the purpose of data usage is limited. The clause is clarified with the following:

{quote}_When Bolt acts as a separate data controller, it collects and processes personal data to provide services under its Terms of Service (_[https://bolt.eu/az-az/legal/|https://bolt.eu/az-az/legal/|smart-link] _) and Privacy Notice (_[https://bolt.eu/az-az/privacy/|https://bolt.eu/az-az/privacy/|smart-link] _)._{quote}

*BLOCK_RIDE_DID_NOT_HAPPEN*

A good portion of the scheduled blocks are related to *commission evasion* on cash orders, particularly in cash-heavy countries.

Overall the blocks seem to work as intended, however with a couple possible false positives.

* +Examples+ [+Driver1+|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/fleet/drivers/edit/679684?tab=profile&filter=%7B%22limit%22%3A25%2C%22offset%22%3A0%2C%22filter%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&tz=Europe%2FLondon], [+Driver2+|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/fleet/drivers/edit/2592838?tab=profile&filter=%7B%22limit%22%3A25%2C%22offset%22%3A0%2C%22filter%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&tz=Europe%2FLondon], [+Driver3+|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/fleet/drivers/edit/2644999?tab=profile&filter=%7B%22limit%22%3A25%2C%22offset%22%3A0%2C%22filter%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&tz=Europe%2FLondon] driver accepts and then cancels orders instead of outright rejecting them. And since the driver was a good distance away, it’d be very inconvenient to follow up with an offline cash order.
Possible edit for [+RIDE_TOO_SHORT_TIME+|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/fraud/rules/edit/ridehailing_driver_order_completed_check/RIDE_TOO_SHORT_TIME?tab=script&scriptTab=script_view]. Distance can be increased to decrease risk of potential offline rides not being flagged:



{noformat}environment = getDefaultValue(input, "data.environment", 'LIVE');

duration = getDefaultValue(input, "data.duration", 9999);

min_distance_driver_arrived_to_destination_to_any_stops_in_km = getDefaultValue(input, "query.order.rides.driver_distances.min_distance_driver_arrived_to_destination_to_any_stops_in_km", 9999);

arrived_to_driving = getDefaultValue(input, "query.order.rides.driver_distances.distances_between_locations.arrived_to_driving", 0);

driving_to_destination = getDefaultValue(input, "query.order.rides.driver_distances.distances_between_locations.driving_to_destination", 0);

driven_distance = arrived_to_driving + driving_to_destination;

cash = match(payment_type, 'default');
campaign_code = getDefaultValue(input, "order.campaign_code", null);

no_campaign = (match(campaign_code, null));

client_vs_driver_dest = getDistance(arrived_to_destination_lat, arrived_to_destination_lng, destination_lat, destination_lng);

client_vs_driver_dest_aux = client_vs_driver_dest != null ? client_vs_driver_dest : false;

good_driver_reject = cash and no_campaign and client_vs_driver_dest_aux >= 1 and duration <= 10;

ride_happened = (driven_distance >= 500 and min_distance_driver_arrived_to_destination_to_any_stops_in_km <= 0.5) or (driven_distance >= 3000 and min_distance_driver_arrived_to_destination_to_any_stops_in_km <= 1);

too_short = duration >= 0 and duration <= 30;

strEquals(environment,'LIVE') and too_short and not(ride_happened) and not (good_driver_reject){noformat}

Could consider that some drivers are not actually trying to abuse the app, and simply don’t know why they were blocked. If not done already - CS could remind drivers regaring proper app use, for drivers who’s scheduled blocks are about to end, or have them pass retraining.

However, for drivers with scheduled blocks of 8 weeks, that’s almost 2 months of being blocked, for some cases it’s people’s 2 month salary gone. 

If they continue doing bad orders after that and “trigger a 16 week block”, they should be hard-blocked instead - not likely to provide good supply.




*BLOCK_REPETITIVE_RIDER_ORDER*

~ 75% of long block cases are due to repeated campaign abuse involving self cash orders. The blocking is functioning as intended, and seemingly no changes are necessary. Only one instance involved legitimate business rides, which is now likely resolved via profiling query update.

